["The first auxiliary has already moved. Supply **been + -ing** after the subject.","The first auxiliary is already at the front. After they, keep **been + sweeping**.","Choose **been sweeping**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The linking auxiliary been is missing.","B":"The first auxiliary is already at the front. After they, keep **been + sweeping**.","C":"The perfect auxiliary requires been, not being.","D":"The active continuous part requires -ing, not the base form."}}},{"id":"b3043c56-f78e-5823-ab0f-414b6efdebfa","topicSlug":"past-perfect-continuous","level":"B2","prompt":"Choose the past perfect SIMPLE, focusing on completion: I ____ the kitchen floor before lunch yesterday.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"had to sweep"},{"id":"B","text":"had swept"},{"id":"C","text":"had been sweeping"},{"id":"D","text":"had sweeping"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Separate **completion** from **duration**. The requested form is perfect simple.","explanation":"The instruction focuses on a completed result, so use the simple perfect **had swept**.","keywords":["simple-continuous-contrast"],"skill":"simple-continuous-contrast","contextKey":"past-perfect-continuous-50","fingerprint":"d99125fef94e79efb69836812b9d50d4d4b71e83a83252362f2634d6f8949f68","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-past-perfect"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Separate **completion** from **duration**. The requested form is perfect simple.","The instruction focuses on a completed result, so use the simple perfect **had swept**.","Choose **had swept**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Have as a perfect auxiliary cannot take a to-infinitive.","B":"The instruction focuses on a completed result, so use the simple perfect **had swept**.","C":"This is perfect continuous, which foregrounds the activity rather than the requested simple form.","D":"This omits been and is not a complete perfect continuous form."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["The past perfect continuous describes an activity leading up to a past moment. It can explain a past condition: someone was tired because they had been working. The simple perfect often foregrounds completion; the continuous foregrounds the activity and its duration.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Focus on the activity","explanation":"The past perfect continuous describes an activity leading up to a past moment. It can explain a past condition: someone was tired because they had been working. The simple perfect often foregrounds completion; the continuous foregrounds the activity and its duration.","pattern":"Subject + **had been + -ing**","commonMistakes":["A continuous form does not guarantee that the activity is unfinished; context can show that it recently stopped."],"examples":["Use the past perfect continuous: She **had been preparing** the visitor guide for an hour before lunch yesterday.","Use the past perfect continuous: She **had been restoring** the old portrait for an hour before lunch yesterday."],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Keep both auxiliaries","explanation":"The construction has three verb parts: **had or had**, then **been**, then an **-ing** form. Only the first auxiliary changes with tense and subject. Been is the past participle of be; it connects perfect aspect with the continuous construction.","pattern":"She **had been working**.","commonMistakes":["Do not omit been or change it to being in this construction."],"examples":["Make a past-perfect-continuous negative: We **had not been preparing** the visitor guide all morning before lunch yesterday.","Make a past-perfect-continuous negative: We **had not been restoring** the old portrait all morning before lunch yesterday."],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Questions and negatives","explanation":"Move only **had or had** before the subject in questions. Put not after that same auxiliary in negatives. Been and the -ing form remain in place. A short answer repeats only the first auxiliary.","pattern":"Had they **been working**?","commonMistakes":["Have been they working? puts too much of the verb phrase before the subject."],"examples":["Complete the past-perfect-continuous question: Had they **been preparing** the visitor guide for long before lunch yesterday?","Complete the past-perfect-continuous question: Had they **been restoring** the old portrait for long before lunch yesterday?"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"State verbs and completed quantities","explanation":"Know, own and believe usually describe states and favour perfect simple: I have known her for years. A completed count also favours the simple: I have written three reports. Repeated activity can use continuous: I have been writing reports all morning. The meaning of the verb matters, not just a time expression.","pattern":"I have **known** her for years. / I have **been waiting** for an hour.","commonMistakes":["For and since can accompany simple perfect states; they do not automatically require continuous."],"examples":["Choose the past perfect SIMPLE, focusing on completion: I **had prepared** the visitor guide before lunch yesterday.","Choose the past perfect SIMPLE, focusing on completion: I **had restored** the old portrait before lunch yesterday."],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["Subject + **had been + -ing**","She **had been working**.","Had they **been working**?","I have **known** her for years. / I have **been waiting** for an hour."]},"stage":"3 · Time and experience","prerequisites":["past-perfect"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) 